TY  - CONF
TI  - Emotion AI in Job Interviews: Injustice, Emotional Labor, Identity, and Privacy
AU  - Ingber, Alexis Shore
AU  - Andalibi, Nazanin
PY  - 2025
AB  - The use of emotion artificial intelligence to evaluate job-seekers during the interview process raises concerns about injustice, particularly among minoritized individuals. However, we know little about how job-seekers experience emotion AI interviews compared to non-emotion AI or human resources (HR) interviews. We address this gap through a 3(Evaluation Source: Emotion AI, AI, HR) x 2(Outcome Favorability: Recommended, Not Recommended) experiment. Participants (n = 456) underwent a mock interview assuming real-time evaluation. We find those who partook an interview evaluated by emotion AI perceived significantly lower levels of justice than that evaluated by HR, with this relationship partially dependent on outcome favorability. Additionally, identity attributes (e.g., gender, race, disability) and privacy concerns significantly influenced the information disclosed and emotional labor exerted during the interview. We discuss practical implications for emotion AI use in job interviews as well as broader methodological implications for capturing real-time engagement with AI systems.
