TI  - The Problem of Generics in LLM Training
AU  - Smith, Becca
AU  - Tolbert, Alexander Williams
PY  - 2025
AB  - Pejorative or harmful stereotypical language appears in large language model (LLM) outputs, and despite various mitigation approaches — such as reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) or manual fixes after red teaming — these issues persist. This paper argues that generics, which are generalizations lacking explicit quantification (e.g., statements like “mosquitoes carry malaria” or “birds lay eggs”), contribute significantly to these harmful stereotypes. Whereas humans naturally contextualize and quantify such generalizations (e.g., discerning that only female birds lay eggs), LLMs may misinterpret these vague expressions. Given the frequency of generics in natural language, if LLMs cannot correctly interpret or quantify them, there exists a potential for persistent, harmful stereotypical outputs. This work employs a philosophy of language approach to show that deficiencies in processing generics can yield problematic assumptions and to demonstrate that current LLMs lack robust mechanisms to handle these ambiguities.
